   "date": "2015-06-10T12:40:03Z",
   "subject": "Doubt regarding estimation of standard error",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello Phil,<br><br>I got two part question here. First, For dichotomous and polytomous data which method of standard error estimates between &#39;crossprod&#39;  and &#39;complete&#39; is better as far as precision  is concerned?<div><br>Second, I was using &#39;crossprod&#39; for dichotomous  data under 3PL model.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> itemtype </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;3PL&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TOL</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.01</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> SE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br> <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Iteration</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">60</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Lik</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">211043.855</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Change</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.00312</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br><br>  <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Calculating</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> information matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br><br></span><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><font color=\"#ff0000\"><span style=\"color:#606\">Warning</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> message</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> loadESTIMATEinfo</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">info </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tmp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ESTIMATE </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ESTIMATE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> constrain </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> constrain</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Could</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">not</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> invert information matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model likely </span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">not</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> identified</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span></font></span></div></code></div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I got above warning message and stand error were not shown.<br><br>whereas when I used &#39;complete&#39; method on the same data, standard errors were shown and no warning messages were displayed.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>why is that?<br><br><br><br>Thanking you,<br><br>Irshad</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "KptrLv7XNoAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2015-06-10T15:45:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: Doubt regarding estimation of standard error",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">You never want complete, it is the information matrix matrix from the complete data solution (which is overly conservative, because it assumes you know the values of theta...which you don&#39;t). You probably want &#39;Louis&#39; instead, which should be more accurate since its the actual observed information matrix (the crossprod is an approximation to this, but its very cheap to compute). <div><br></div><div>That being said, good luck getting a positive definite information matrix for the 3PL model, You sample size must be *very* large for this to occur, which is one of the downsides of this model. Cheers.</div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
